 Yeah, some type of agua.

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:59.040
 But the inspiration for that meal was the, and for doing, for basically honoring the legacy of the free breakfast program is the Black Panther Party for self-defense.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:12.100
 That was also one of the threads that we shared in common was just this fascination, love, respect, admiration for the work of the Black Panther Party, the roots in Oakland, and the survival programs in particular.

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:17.300
 So the meal, which is now going on how many years that we've been doing it?

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.380
 This will be our eighth year.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:29.800
 With Life is Living is to serve specifically the youth that come to participate in the programs, including Young Gifted in Black and so many other amazing youth performers.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.920
 And to really kind of kick off that weekend.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:35.490
 And it's held in Defermary Park.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:51.870
 And Defermary Park, also known as Little Bobby Hutton Park, is also this incredible site for organizing, for a lot, for conferences, for survival conferences, for sickle cell testing, for breakfast, for so many of those programs that the Black Panther Party had in the 60s and 70s.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:10:04.850
 And so it's really, you know, it's really like, you know, after I started cooking with these folks, I remember I found like a physical old style, like file folder that I had of like clippings that I collected over the years about the free breakfast program.

00:10:04.850 --> 00:10:18.130
 And, you know, just like the head of the FBI calling it the single most dangerous threat to domestic security was like feeding young black children.

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:23.600
 And at one point, I mean, the statistic is that they were feeding more children throughout the country than the entire state of California.

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:28.170
 And that's why we have free breakfast and lunch programs in schools now, right?

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:34.170
 Because the Panthers shamed the government basically into providing breakfast and lunch in schools.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:53.120
 And that was really like what the, not just the genius of the Panthers, but that was one of the driving factors between a lot of their survival programs was they were, they termed them survival pending revolution, meaning this is, this is just to get us from point A to point B and make sure that we stay alive to get to the revolution, right?

00:10:53.120 --> 00:11:04.690
 And these were all programs that were coming out of necessity and they weren't like grant funded and they weren't, you know, they were people need like things like shoes for homeless people program, like it's winter and it's cold.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:05.690
 People need shoes.

00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:18.800
 We are going to make sure there's a way for people to get shoes or like they had a program of, you know, their own ambulance services in their communities because ambulances weren't going to serve their communities and didn't and didn't answer calls.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:30.760
 And they had their own like patrols because cops only came into their communities to beat up and shoot their community and not to actually like serve and protect or whatever that cops are supposed to do.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:52.580
 Then there is, you know, and they had the visiting the elders and making sure people cared for intended for like these are all programs that are taken for granted and more well to do neighborhoods and I think in more privileged communities and for the Panthers to one lay claim to that and also create infrastructure to make sure that was possible was and is a revolutionary act.

00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:58.720
 So something that comes up for me is each of you is pretty formidable in your own right, right?

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.870
 Like you do a lot of different things in your life.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:05.320
 Um, but what does it mean to like represent a legacy?

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:22.340
 Like it's when I've seen you guys and when I've been in your space in your spaces and eating at your meals, which I don't know how this happened to everybody, but I feel like I went to every PKC meal for four or five months in a row and then it was accidental, but I just kept finding myself there.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:25.340
 Did you get your punch card?

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:26.340
 Seriously.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:27.340
 Did you get your punch card?

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:29.340
 What happened to me?

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.340
 Wouldn't you like to know?

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:39.380
 Um, what does it mean to represent a legacy?

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:46.380
 You don't just rep, I mean, you're talking about the Panthers right now, but my sense is that you represent lots of legacies and that you...

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:47.510
 It's, that's really true.

00:12:47.510 --> 00:13:10.770
 And I'm glad you bring it up that way because, you know, we are incredibly, oh my gosh, it's, it's an incredible privilege to be able to represent so much legacy amongst the three of us, uh, representing, uh, so many diasporic traditions, um, and ancestry is so key to the way that we're able to show up together as a collective.

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:20.440
 Um, you know, one of the quotes that's sort of just really moving around in my, my brain and my heart right now is this idea of the opposite of poverty isn't wealth.

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:22.350
 The opposite of poverty is justice.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:47.730
 And so no matter what tradition we're pulling from in our own familiar tradition, familial traditions or experiences professionally, there's something that is resonating around this idea that we have everything we need within community and within our, um, the wisdom of our elders to heal ourselves and to survive and to create a path forward for black and brown folks and their lived experience.

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:57.730
 Uh, that is joyful, uh, that is joyful, uh, that is in sort of that place of claiming the decolonized reality of our present, you know, uh, sociopolitical moment.

00:13:57.730 --> 00:14:10.730
 Uh, so it's really, it's rich to be able to do that through this idea of, or through the reality of, uh, food traditions, through creativity, uh, and through active social justice by cooking.

00:14:10.730 --> 00:14:20.350
 I've been thinking a lot as an artist of what gets preserved, like what gets included and what gets left out and, you know, and the histories behind that, like whose story gets told, you know, and, and how.

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:34.350
 And so I think to think about the legacy of the Black Panthers, you know, I take my students, uh, at CCA on this walking tour of the area in North Auckland and they're always shocked at like, why isn't there even a plaque here?

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.090
 Like, why isn't there a statue here?

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:38.430
 Like, why isn't there, like, why is this so invisible?

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.270
 You know, and the reasons to that are very complicated and there are many.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:45.300
 It goes back to the same, you know, like why, why was the, why were the Panthers so threatening?

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:52.740
 Um, you know, and, and I think that that invisibility, I see that to see the opportunity kind of where the challenge is.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:15:00.980
 You've been thinking a lot about like, okay, there's, there's two ways to preserve something, you know, that you can take, you can take it, you can put it behind glass, you can stick in a museum, you can forget about it.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:01.200
 Right.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:04.930
 Or you can learn how to make the thing and you can recreate it.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:18.370
 And it's, I think communities of color in particular, um, indigenous communities throughout the world have placed a lot of importance on passing on the knowledge of how to do the thing instead of just preserving object and moving on to something else.

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:26.250
 And I feel like cooking is one of those crafts that, you know, it is informed by what came before us very specifically.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:42.380
 And that's, I think, one of the reasons that I find cooking a way to like keep these histories alive and keep these like, you know, as an extension of, of this legacy, whether that's Black Panther Party, whether, you know, that's migration of my own people coming from different continents.

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:45.600
 But it's something, you know, it's not passive.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:49.830
 Like you can, when you're sitting at a table, like you even have to decide, like, am I going to eat this?

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:50.690
 Am I not going to eat this?

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:53.110
 You know, like, it's a very active process.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:59.910
 And I think that that's part of the opportunity that we see in engaging people is like, this is history alive and this is past, present and future all at the same time.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:03.850
 Because that type of like white supremacy, you know, thrives in silence, right?

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:10.290
 And thrives in us not knowing our own histories, white supremacy and a myriad of systems of oppression.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:26.250
 So this act of like keeping and being able to talk about the Panthers or the Brown Berets or the Red Guard or the Guider Party or the American Indian movement, like all these political movements actively to talk about them as if, you know, and talk about the present day impacts on our lives.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:36.480
 I think it's very powerful and political, but to talk about side by side with our immigrant stories and immigration stories and people's migration histories is very powerful, too.

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:46.240
 Because it reminds us both in the food that we cook, but also in our day-to-day lives that, you know, one, we come from giants and two, we are also giants, right?

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:59.200
 That things that our ancestors have done that at the time seemed formidable and impossible have been done and that we have that connection to that.

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:04.160
 And we've got the recipes that fed them as they went through all of that, right?

00:17:05.010 --> 00:17:10.910
 And so for us, I think that as we're cooking, it's constantly trying to reclaim that, like the power, the strength.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:25.760
 And like the Panthers demonstrated so often, the self-determination of being able to cook and feed and gather people together around a meal and just the immense politic of that.

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:32.740
 So At Your Meals, the ones that I've been to have started with an invocation of your ancestors.

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:37.300
 And I'm thinking about Marvin K. White and his...

00:17:37.300 --> 00:17:38.000
 Theopoetics.

00:17:38.620 --> 00:17:44.250
 Theopoetics and the invitation to have prophetic space as part of the work that you do.

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:53.020
 And something for me feels really different and really powerful about work that is both inward-looking and outward-looking at the same time.

00:17:53.520 --> 00:18:02.740
 There's something that feels like, like I leave and I feel really fed and I feel really nourished because I was part of ritual and because I'm full.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:14.660
 I'm satiated in this way that is different than a fancy dinner out or is, that feels more like my hope for what family party feel like.

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:20.790
 I may say that because we were talking about this before we started recording.

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:25.830
 But, you know, where there's a coming together and there's a celebration and there's a nuance.

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:33.550
 And I wonder if you guys can talk about this idea of invocation and the prophetic in your work.

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:38.440
 I think it's so interesting because Marvin, one of the things he also says is that, you know, this is for us.

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:43.160
 And just remember, like, this is for, you know, this is for us.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:52.880
 And I think that inward-outward is like, you know, from the folks who are participating and producing the food and putting it on a table to like, you know, we're having a really good time.

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:54.920
 Even before the guests show up.

00:18:55.410 --> 00:19:07.370
 And I also think that one of the ways that we invoke is, to me, it's Jocelyn, you're singing, is part of that invocation and part of just like that feeling that you can't get from spoken words otherwise.

00:19:07.370 --> 00:19:08.550
 It only comes through song.

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:11.240
 That really also feels like a deep ancestral tie.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:21.960
 And that goes back to what Sita does, which is bring this really strong attention to the artistry of the moment, the composition of an evening in order to be experienced.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:39.840
 And there's something really true in, beyond the spoken word, if there is poetry present, if there is song present, if there is an altar present that has the spirit of elders with it.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:54.670
 If smudging has happened in the room with the intention of us all being empowered and uplifted by the presence of our ancestries, that creates a ground for us to stand on for every event that is beyond us and for us all at the same time.

00:19:55.200 --> 00:19:57.280
 And that's all on purpose.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.040
 You know, that's no mistake in that.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:10.950
 There are things that we do every time intentionally in order to create a space that everyone can be invited into, to have a sense of welcome, so that they can be changed during the process of an evening.

00:20:10.950 --> 00:20:21.120
 It's something that really matters for me to create an environment where folks really do believe and feel a sense of beloved community.

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:27.700
 That there is a shift of when you're outside the door and when you come in the room.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:40.100
 And for that to be so palpable and so moving that we can create an environment of transformation and of we are living the reality that we actually want to have in the world.

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:45.930
 Without those opportunities to experience that, for it to be lived, it doesn't seem possible in the world.

00:20:46.610 --> 00:20:54.760
 And so for that to happen, and every time for me it feels like it happens, it's so delicious to be able to have that experience.

00:20:55.420 --> 00:21:06.620
 And that's why it's so, it's sometimes very disorienting because we are in this, we are in this sort of a pressure cooker of getting an event done and all that that includes and all the directions that we're moving.

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:10.520
 And then there's those moments where we all come together and we can see what's happening.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:18.060
 And it's just remarkable to know that there's frenzy, there's chaos of just, you know, eventness.

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:30.510
 But there's also this solid thing that's happening in that space of a connection of one human being to another in order to elicit a more beautiful and joyful reality for all of us to exist in.

00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:32.290
 Like nothing short of that, you know.

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:33.910
 That's what I want to do on a daily.

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:35.530
 I don't have time for anything else.

00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:39.140
 So I'm really grateful that PKC can create that, you know, for our community.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:39.600
 Oh.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:40.160
 Yeah.

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:45.300
 I like that you said, like how you felt filled by it, by that meal, right?

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:53.280
 So like one of our sayings with PKC is it's not just to fill your stomach, but it's also to nourish your soul, feed your mind and fuel the movement, right?

00:21:53.280 --> 00:22:02.070
 That we want the meals not just to be about sitting and eating because the meal extends so far beyond just the sitting and eating together.

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:08.870
 That we want that to feed you and fill you, but carry you out to the streets, advocate for the issues that matter, right?

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:13.110
 That it uses all of your senses to engage in the PKC space.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:19.170
 And that for us is very intentional and very particular of how we design the space and it's part of that ritual, right?

00:22:19.170 --> 00:22:33.370
 Trying to move something, people's relationship to eating, especially in the Bay Area with this very mainstream foodie movement that's so much about consumption and conspicuous consumption of look at my place in life by where I'm eating and how much I'm eating.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:41.760
 What I'm spending on this meal to flip that and part of this act of decolonization of how to remove that type of consumption from it.

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:49.960
 So if we bring it back to this, like you said, this family space or this familial space where we can care for, tend to and build relationship in a more genuine way.

00:22:50.300 --> 00:22:59.310
 And I think a lot of that for us and one of the things I love most about PKC is that it prioritizes and centers the voices and experiences of people of color.

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:10.220
 And it tells and brings together narratives and stories of people often left out of the mainstream food movement and outside of the foodie narratives.

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:12.900
 This food was never meant for us to eat.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:20.880
 The style of gathering, you know, governments have done a lot to keep us from being able to gather and have common spaces like this.

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:32.680
 So again, this feels a little bit like a survival program in and of itself of how do we sit, how do we come together, how do we nourish ourselves and feed our movements.

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:39.490
 Yeah, you said you'd started with invocation and I never made this connection between invocation and hospitality.

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:45.830
 And I think that one of the things that I'm reminded of is just in my family.

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:49.430
 I mean, hospitality meant that you knew that you had to serve your elders first.

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:57.840
 And there was, you know, that there is an there's an ancestry that you have to understand in order to be able to provide hospitality.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:04.530
 And I think that that's what gets divorced when you think of hospitality is like something that I can go and pay for.

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:04.870
 Right.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:12.250
 You know, that it's a service that and I think that one of the things that we say in our meals is that you are you are in service.

00:24:13.360 --> 00:24:14.520
 You are you are being of service.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:15.220
 You're being sorry.

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:16.120
 You're being of service.

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:18.120
 You're not a server server server.

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:18.460
 Right.

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:36.920
 And, you know, and it's complicated because there's a lot of patriarchy, a lot of class that is embedded in those codes, you know, and I think that for me, PKC is also a space to figure out like, OK, like, what is it that we of those?

00:24:37.980 --> 00:24:42.830
 You know, because the ancestors like grateful for them and they didn't have all the answers in the world.

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:44.450
 It's changed a lot in that time, you know.

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:53.240
 So, like, how do we how do we like take the best of how they survived and thrived and also make it our own?

00:24:53.960 --> 00:24:54.100
 Right.

00:24:54.220 --> 00:24:56.200
 And prepare ourselves for what's to come.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:56.560
 Yeah.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:25:15.200
 Because there's a lot of the I mean, this process we've been doing PKC now for a while and it's always been this process of creating new language also around food work and restaurant work and, you know, creating a new operating model of what a kitchen can be and how a food business can operate.

00:25:15.200 --> 00:25:21.660
 And it's been a constant learning process, but also designing process and unlearning process.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:24.220
 We could talk about that for the rest of the time.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.750
 There's so much structural to delve into with that.

00:25:28.390 --> 00:25:36.140
 One thing that stands out to me is this continual process of visibility that you encourage in your in your process.

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:43.560
 You talk about what goes into the meal every time, right, as a process, as a dynamic action rather than as a product.

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:44.100
 Right.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:47.740
 And I think there's this really humanizing quality about that.

00:25:47.740 --> 00:25:55.410
 And I think visibility, as I experience it at your meals, is about me being visible to myself, too.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:56.310
 Right.

00:25:56.430 --> 00:26:02.650
 And so I think of all these tropes, like there's a space for everybody at the table and all of these things.

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:07.060
 It's like, what does that actually feel like to have space for everybody?

00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:27.140
 I think every part of your process from your sourcing to how people are working in your kitchen to the way that food is served to the spaces that you decide to inhabit are about holding this visibility in a way that notices where you are and the complexity that it holds.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:31.530
 So I'm curious, y'all are a pretty intentional group, right?

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:36.250
 Like, there's not a lot that happens by accident with these three minds sitting at the table.

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:36.830
 What?

00:26:38.710 --> 00:26:41.590
 There's stuff that happens by accident, but then we figure out why.

00:26:41.730 --> 00:26:41.870
 Yeah.

00:26:43.190 --> 00:26:45.770
 They throw some language on it.

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:46.390
 You're like, yeah.

00:26:46.890 --> 00:26:47.110
 Yeah.

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:48.170
 That's pretty good.

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:48.450
 Yeah.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:49.370
 That's good.

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:50.290
 Sorry.

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:51.450
 What was the rest of your question?

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:51.670
 No, that's it.

00:26:51.770 --> 00:26:54.140
 I mean, so what is visibility?

00:26:54.360 --> 00:27:05.000
 You know, it really brings to mind what was in our consideration when creating this four-part meal series that we were mentioning before, from the farm to the kitchen to the table to the street.

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:24.760
 And when we were conceptualizing each of those spaces and what it means to be transparent about what or who the wisdom keepers are in each of those places and spaces, what was really resonant for the first one, farm, was in some ways surprising.

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:29.030
 Because for farm, it really was trying to capture the feeling of home.

00:27:29.030 --> 00:27:42.610
 And the feeling of home is a way of claiming land and honoring the things that come from the land, which, you know, most obviously is food production, you know, where we source our food from.

00:27:42.950 --> 00:27:44.410
 But it's also where we get our flavors.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:55.290
 Without the land, without it being a vessel for the flavors of our homelands, we aren't actually able to engage with any other part of the meal process or experience.

00:27:55.930 --> 00:27:57.330
 And that's critical.

00:27:57.330 --> 00:28:05.420
 But in our farm to table ethic, farm is reduced to a place of production instead of process.

00:28:06.170 --> 00:28:10.030
 The stories are eliminated from the commercialization of the production of food.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:22.440
 And so for us to reclaim the space as something that is deeper and richer and filled with basically the blood of our ancestors, that's a beautiful experience.

00:28:22.440 --> 00:28:28.370
 And that is, like you say, there is a lot of intention here.

00:28:28.590 --> 00:28:32.530
 To be able to evoke the feeling of what I described, you know, it seems impossible.

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:33.910
 And in many ways it is.

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:51.140
 But the fact that we make the attempt to bring that into a space of where a meal is being shared by community makes me so happy that we can even try, you know, to do that in the face of so many systems that are trying to limit the stories that we're being able to share.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:55.800
 Or remember or create on behalf of our own survival.

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:07.160
 I think there's so much in the mainstream foodie culture and restaurant culture that thrives on making people invisible and making our labor invisible, right?

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:08.520
 So I really love hearing that.

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:13.860
 That's something that you noticed at our meal that struck you as being specific.

00:29:13.860 --> 00:29:22.280
 Because it is something that's come up, you know, people's kitchen is 12 years old now, about 11 or 12 years old.

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:24.780
 And our collective is 10 years old.

00:29:25.010 --> 00:29:26.490
 It feels so much older.

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:27.790
 50.

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:28.970
 50 years old.

00:29:29.350 --> 00:29:33.270
 And then two years or one year for our collective, something like that.

00:29:33.290 --> 00:29:34.190
 A little over here now.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:34.590
 Yeah.

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:37.570
 I am not the memory keeper in this collective.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:43.620
 But so, you know, we've done this so many different ways over this time period.

00:29:44.140 --> 00:29:57.060
 But one of the things that's always been consistent is that the people who make the food should be the center of the narrative and be brought out and also been and be engaged with and given thanks to in a way that restaurants don't do it.

00:29:57.060 --> 00:30:12.370
 Right. Because if you go to most restaurants, regardless of how much you're paying, regardless of how foodie or how many stars it is or how local it is, almost every time, the darker the skin color of the person, the further back of house they're going to be.

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:15.050
 Right. Even that distinction of front of house to back of house.

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:21.910
 With restaurants built, the modern day restaurant built off of the French military, right, and taken to design from the French military.

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:31.940
 There's a lot of bullshit and horrible, horrible politic that's kind of built into the way that restaurants operate and the way that they thrive.

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.980
 So for us to do that is definitely very intentional.

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:42.600
 Again, this thing about narrative and story and experiences, what it looks like to center the experiences of people of color in this.

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:55.420
 When Jocelyn was talking about from the farm to the kitchen, to the table, to the street, we're focusing on each of those sites because each of those are sites of knowledge production, culture keeping, storytelling, and places of survival for folks of color.

00:30:55.580 --> 00:31:03.740
 But it's also the parts of the food narrative that we're left out of the most, you know, for folks of color, for immigrant people, our experiences exist on the narrative.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:20.820
 So if the narrative is just farm to table, we are on the narrative, we are the, the folks of color are the people in between that who are bringing the food from the farm to the table or working at the farm or taking it away from the table back to the dish room and then to compost or to the processing plant.

00:31:20.820 --> 00:31:36.860
 Right. And that is something that I feel like when you go out to eat simply as a consumer and you're just paying to put this experience, you know, this meal into your body, you're also paying to not have to think about all the other people that have just been invisibilized from your narrative.

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:41.860
 So you could feel good about this meal. Right. You're paying for that privilege to feel good.

00:31:41.980 --> 00:31:51.580
 Yeah. I think about this because we, you know, we speak on college campuses and I remember going up to take in my, somebody had invited me to eat, you know, in the cafeteria with them.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:32:06.730
 And there's literally a little window and you can kind of see people through the slats, you know, of the, the conveyor belt turning and you put your tray down and it's all folks of color scraping plates, you know, at their eight hour shift.

00:32:06.730 --> 00:32:11.030
 And like that, you know, and it's like, who is on the other end of that wall?

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:12.090
 Don't worry about it, Sita.

00:32:13.270 --> 00:32:13.710
 Yeah.

00:32:19.310 --> 00:32:49.280
 Yeah. I mean, we have to laugh, right? Cause it's like, so not funny. But that, that is, it's that invisibility. And I think one of the things that stood out to me, Jocelyn, that you said at the farm meal was, you said for some of us, the farm was a plantation. And that's why it's important for us to reclaim this space and this land. And I just like, I thought that to me, when I heard you say that, I was like, and that's why we're different than a restaurant. That's why we're different than, you know, like, this is how PKC makes space.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:33:00.550
 It's for the story that you would not pay $75 to sit in here, you know, on a casual Tuesday night. We'd like to change that actually.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:03.950
 Yeah. If you want to pay more farm meals, we would definitely welcome that.

00:33:04.890 --> 00:33:05.330
 P.S.

00:33:05.510 --> 00:33:05.950
 P.S.

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:12.010
 So, Sakim, I think you talked about this idea of a new language.

00:33:13.240 --> 00:33:17.680
 And now all three of you have mentioned a narrative and a story.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:37.580
 And as you were talking, I was thinking about, you know, these places in between and what, why it feels so different to me about this as opposed to kind of like straight up activist work is that it's not oppositional in a simple way, right?

00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:45.390
 Everybody exists in this narrative of the in between, like everybody understands marginality in some way.

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:48.700
 When it gets complicated, it touches everybody, right?

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:49.940
 Food touches everybody.

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:57.300
 And when you tell the story over and over again of, can you guys tell me again, farm to kitchen, to kitchen, to table, to the street.

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:01.230
 Those are spaces we know as people, right?

00:34:01.230 --> 00:34:12.320
 And I think as people of color, you're highlighting the ways that these systems of oppression make it so we don't know each other's stories, right?

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:16.740
 How there's, because there's these little silos in that, those destinations create these silos.

00:34:17.040 --> 00:34:22.190
 And that process in between feels like, feels like the misconnections on Craigslist.

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:25.610
 But it feels like, you know, it feels, there's some softness in there.

00:34:25.730 --> 00:34:35.460
 There's, there's these ways that we touch each other, both in human ways and, and as movement builders and as activists.

00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:38.610
 And it imagines something different for a moment.

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:43.830
 So I guess, what was it like to start to tell the story together?

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:48.350
 Every time I see the three of you, I can tell some conversations have happened.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:51.370
 Are you on our WhatsApp thread?

00:34:52.430 --> 00:34:53.110
 It's fierce.

00:34:53.910 --> 00:34:55.690
 Hundreds of messages a day.

00:34:56.870 --> 00:34:58.190
 Talk about archiving.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:00.050
 Oh, man.

00:35:00.590 --> 00:35:03.190
 I recently saw a book that was all WhatsApp threads.

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:03.950
 Oh, God.

00:35:04.330 --> 00:35:04.510
 Yeah.

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:05.810
 It was really, it was great.

00:35:06.090 --> 00:35:06.210
 I'm sure.

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:06.670
 Yeah.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:08.470
 But anyway, it's not suggesting you do that.

00:35:08.750 --> 00:35:11.430
 But no, but what's it like to come up with a new language?

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:17.080
 You know, what is it like to come up with a new language?

00:35:17.410 --> 00:35:19.590
 This is something I talk about a lot.

00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:25.290
 Because Nodra Lorde quote is around the fact that not only is it our, our work to be activists,

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:32.330
 but it's our work to create language that can actually describe our identities and what we're

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:34.210
 up to because that language doesn't exist.

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:42.190
 And when that's the case, it feels like a super huge amount of creativity is needed to constantly

00:35:42.190 --> 00:35:51.780
 reside in a constantly shifting environment of ideas and the sacred and, you know, the tangible,

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:55.290
 you know, realities of surviving.

00:35:56.220 --> 00:36:03.540
 And what actually comes to mind is interesting is our Kitchen Remedies project, where that's

00:36:03.540 --> 00:36:06.980
 going to be sort of the basis of our next meal, our kitchen meal.

00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:17.210
 We had this opportunity to interact with thousands of people around the question of what were the

00:36:17.210 --> 00:36:21.010
 remedies that your family gave to you when you weren't feeling well.

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:24.470
 And we created this, you know, this prescription pad.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:33.950
 And it has some really, you know, sort of mundane illnesses like headaches and stomach aches,

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:38.970
 but it also has homophobia and capitalism and white supremacy.

00:36:38.970 --> 00:36:43.330
 It's like, what does it take to heal these social ills as well?

00:36:44.250 --> 00:36:48.650
 And so in this process, we're interacting with people and their stories that they're sharing

00:36:48.650 --> 00:36:49.190
 with us.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:56.170
 And in every context that we have done this project, tears have been elicited because of

00:36:56.170 --> 00:37:01.170
 the depth of that memory that's associated with the process of healing in a family.

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:10.370
 Sometimes they're tears of love, sometimes of sadness, sometimes of the feeling of not being

00:37:10.370 --> 00:37:13.070
 cared for in their, in their childhoods.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:22.130
 It's a really deep investigation into how we are able to support one another in this process

00:37:22.130 --> 00:37:25.840
 of healing physical and social ills.

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:32.410
 But what is important to me in that process is that although we have this really specific

00:37:32.410 --> 00:37:38.340
 thing that has very specific language, so many times in the, in this thousands and thousands

00:37:38.340 --> 00:37:43.950
 of people, you know, interacting with them, there's been the need to break it down even

00:37:43.950 --> 00:37:50.380
 further and to describe even more for specific folks who are like, oh, you know, they have

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:50.880
 a block on.

00:37:50.940 --> 00:37:52.880
 It's like, I've never, I've never had this happen.

00:37:52.960 --> 00:37:55.740
 I've never had a remedy given to me or anything like that.

00:37:56.040 --> 00:38:01.960
 There's a discussion that ensues that gets me to a point of connecting to an individual

00:38:01.960 --> 00:38:06.700
 to hear their story in their own words.

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:08.750
 It wasn't on this sheet.

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:10.600
 There just needed to be human connection.

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:17.380
 There needed to be an opportunity or an invitation to go deeper into what all this means to heal

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:26.320
 oneself, to be healed by one's family, to be in a place of receiving the wisdom of elders.

00:38:26.320 --> 00:38:32.370
 Like that often needs that one-on-one connection to elicit the stories that are their lived experience.

00:38:32.950 --> 00:38:34.050
 And that's phenomenal.

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:36.460
 You know, that's really powerful to me.

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:43.300
 And I'm grateful that PKC as an entity can create projects like that where it insists that

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:49.370
 we get uncomfortable often, but we take the time to have that one-on-one exchange of being

00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:55.430
 really humble of what it is to be human in a universal way, but also in a really nuanced way

00:38:55.430 --> 00:39:03.290
 of each person living a life that is dynamic and insists on being heard like that.

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:10.090
 I think part of what I've been surprised about, I mean, kind of, because we began cooking very

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:14.330
 naturally and kind of did the thing before we even called it, you know, before we even said,

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:15.150
 oh, it's true.

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:16.510
 We are now people's kitchen collector.

00:39:16.570 --> 00:39:17.970
 We had done like three or four meals by then.

00:39:18.630 --> 00:39:22.150
 You know, it kind of, Sacky Black's a laugh because he's like, ha ha, gotcha.

00:39:23.120 --> 00:39:24.320
 Sucker them all into it.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:31.180
 But I think what has surprised me is just the work that happens between the three of us and

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:37.350
 how if we start out with the base recognition that perfection is impossible, nothing is, everything

00:39:37.350 --> 00:39:39.710
 is very far from perfect, but we are intentional.

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:49.030
 And I think that, you know, each time that we create an experience, create an event, like

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:53.850
 we are reflecting back on it and we're thinking about, you know, something new comes up each

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:55.210
 time, something new.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:40:00.870
 And it makes us better, but it's also, you know, I think that for, you know, for me, like

00:40:00.870 --> 00:40:04.830
 I came from a corporate world where there's a lot of finger pointing, a lot of look busy,

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:10.600
 a lot of over communicating, you know, in order to look busy because you're trying to, you

00:40:10.600 --> 00:40:14.680
 know, clock that you're like, you know, whatever, nine to six or whatever.

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:18.150
 And that does not apply in a space.

00:40:18.530 --> 00:40:22.530
 Like we are here for completely different reasons.

00:40:23.030 --> 00:40:26.480
 And I think that that, you know, like, yeah, that's the impulse.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:30.440
 And I think a lot of that is kitchen culture too, you know, of like, I didn't do it.

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:31.220
 It's your fault.

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:36.750
 You know, and I, and I think that we've, we've been even kind of put in that place in different

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:37.150
 events.

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:45.480
 And it's really crazy to me to not go there because it's like, so it's real, it is really

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:45.860
 incredible.

00:40:45.860 --> 00:40:48.920
 Like, I feel like that's one of the really kind of special things about the way that we

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:51.220
 work, you know, that we are really supportive.

00:40:51.340 --> 00:40:56.370
 Like I've showed up an hour and a half late to a meeting before because I fell asleep in

00:40:56.370 --> 00:41:00.190
 my acupuncture appointment, you know, and, and, you know, and, and I think before I would

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:04.960
 have been like, I, you know, making up excuses or whatever, you know, but I like, I, so I

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:09.380
 think that that maybe, and hopefully what you're seeing is like some reflection of that

00:41:09.380 --> 00:41:16.060
 externally that like, that's also, we're learning how to, how to be, um, and how to work, um,

00:41:16.060 --> 00:41:19.980
 as a very small microcosm of, of like the way that we want to work and move in the world.

00:41:20.130 --> 00:41:25.250
 One of my favorite people's kitchen movements was preparing for the free breakfast program

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:31.700
 one year and Rose's son, Darius, who was at that time, I think it was nine.

00:41:31.820 --> 00:41:31.960
 Yeah.

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:32.510
 Yeah.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:37.400
 And Rose is, uh, one of the cooks who works with us all the time and we're like super

00:41:37.400 --> 00:41:40.910
 consistent volunteers, super brilliant, brilliant in the kitchen.

00:41:41.590 --> 00:41:47.060
 And Darius is probably one of the most favorite inspiring kids I've ever met.

00:41:47.380 --> 00:41:52.680
 He, uh, was in the kitchen with us and he was making biscuits with Jocelyn and, and he

00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:55.880
 was asking like, how, who, who's in charge here?

00:41:55.880 --> 00:41:58.120
 Cause he wanted to work with us and he was going to ask for a job.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:41:59.160
 It's like, who's in charge?

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:01.280
 We're like, well, no one's in charge.

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:02.180
 We're a collective.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:07.140
 And we were explaining to him like how we operate as a collective and how do we make decisions

00:42:07.140 --> 00:42:09.240
 in the kitchen horizontally and what that means.

00:42:10.030 --> 00:42:11.350
 And he's like, well, I want to work with you.

00:42:11.490 --> 00:42:13.030
 I said, do you want to be in charge?

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:14.690
 He's like, no, I want to be part of the collective.

00:42:15.650 --> 00:42:19.300
 And it was that very, like, it was that one of those moments where I was like, yes, this

00:42:19.300 --> 00:42:19.640
 is it.

00:42:19.700 --> 00:42:22.610
 Like we don't, you don't need someone in charge, right?

00:42:22.650 --> 00:42:27.050
 There doesn't, when you can build a culture of working together and a culture of care and

00:42:27.050 --> 00:42:27.630
 accountability.

00:42:27.910 --> 00:42:31.960
 One of the things that Sita was saying earlier that struck me and reminded me of this idea

00:42:31.960 --> 00:42:34.380
 of like accepting that, you know, this is not about perfection.

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:39.530
 This is about being in process together and about learning and creating new language and

00:42:39.530 --> 00:42:44.140
 new restaurant culture and new food culture and new ways of cooking.

00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:49.540
 And I think that, you know, perfection and this demand for perfection is this very white

00:42:49.540 --> 00:42:55.240
 supremacist notion and this demand for something to be only a certain way and to have the end

00:42:55.240 --> 00:42:57.920
 product to be exactly like you want it to be.

00:42:57.980 --> 00:43:05.270
 And for us to do away with that and not try to go in that direction, but really like be

00:43:05.270 --> 00:43:10.590
 about the process, like you mentioned earlier, to have people see the process of a meal and

00:43:10.590 --> 00:43:16.300
 to see the food being made over a lot of different ways like that, I think is very powerful and

00:43:16.300 --> 00:43:16.580
 potent.

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:22.860
 I think that's part of this new language of restaurant work where there's so much about

00:43:22.860 --> 00:43:28.700
 like me, I came up and find dining kitchens and working in restaurants and got really indoctrinated

00:43:28.700 --> 00:43:33.600
 with this idea that's very machismo and very patriarchal, very top-down notion of what a

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:34.180
 chef is.

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:39.530
 And that is the overall narrative of foodie culture and chef cultures is very abusive,

00:43:39.970 --> 00:43:42.750
 very top-down, very white male culture.

00:43:42.910 --> 00:43:45.570
 That again comes from the French military, right?

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:52.500
 That has these longstanding roots of demanding, unquestioning loyalty and a lack of critical

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.300
 thinking, just execution, execution.

00:43:54.500 --> 00:43:58.400
 And for us, if you're in the kitchen with us, there's so much conversation.

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:04.610
 There's so much like people thinking together, taking a recipe and creating it together, but

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:06.230
 also putting their own version on it.

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:08.570
 And there isn't this expectation of perfection.

00:44:08.770 --> 00:44:12.170
 There's this expectation of love and care and attention to detail.

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:13.160
 And participation.

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:14.600
 And participation, right?

00:44:14.660 --> 00:44:19.400
 That we want to feel everyone's hands in this and we want it to be a reflection of us in

00:44:19.400 --> 00:44:20.340
 this moment together.

00:44:21.380 --> 00:44:23.920
 And I think that is really beautiful.

00:44:24.320 --> 00:44:29.560
 And one of the things I think that lands most with people that eat with us.

00:44:29.690 --> 00:44:31.230
 It's also really delicious.

00:44:32.660 --> 00:44:33.170
 Thanks.

00:44:33.250 --> 00:44:33.810
 Yeah, thanks.

00:44:34.070 --> 00:44:41.190
 I mean, I've eaten many meals that many hands have touched that are not delicious, right?

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:43.310
 So I think that's a challenge.

00:44:43.610 --> 00:44:50.160
 To me, that feels like the work of building like a beloved community is the work of building

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:51.500
 a beloved community, right?

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:53.380
 Where it's not a destination.

00:44:53.800 --> 00:44:55.100
 It's a dynamic process.

00:44:55.800 --> 00:44:59.740
 And there's moments in that, right?

00:45:00.590 --> 00:45:05.600
 I'm thinking about Food Not Bombs, which we talked a lot with Kristen Leach about, which

00:45:05.600 --> 00:45:11.060
 seems to be this common thread that comes up with a lot of people and how it's distinctly

00:45:11.060 --> 00:45:12.660
 not delicious ever, actually.

00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:14.060
 It's like almost inedible.

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:14.680
 Yeah.

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:18.760
 Let me be clear, because I don't want it to sound like everything I said is too woo-woo.

00:45:19.920 --> 00:45:22.400
 If the food isn't good, we won't serve it, right?

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:24.260
 We're still a food project.

00:45:24.260 --> 00:45:27.160
 And I will be the first to know that your food tastes like trash.

00:45:27.580 --> 00:45:28.300
 I love you.

00:45:28.360 --> 00:45:32.490
 I don't care how much love and attention to detail and care and what wishes you put into

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:32.750
 this.

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:35.990
 What you should have put into it is some salt and some spices.

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:39.550
 And that, you know, like I've been in a lot of kitchens like that.

00:45:39.610 --> 00:45:40.590
 Like, oh, I care about you.

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:43.670
 Like, you obviously don't, because this does not taste like that.

00:45:44.370 --> 00:45:48.430
 And that, I think, is the real family part of it, of being in the kitchen together.

00:45:48.430 --> 00:45:52.510
 And I know I have family kids, like cooking with my grandma and my aunts.

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:54.710
 Like, I've made a lot of bad things.

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:58.260
 And they will be the first to tell me to my face, like, you were disappointed.

00:45:59.200 --> 00:46:00.220
 This is really bad.

00:46:00.380 --> 00:46:02.260
 But I believe something else that happens.

00:46:03.420 --> 00:46:06.260
 Jocelyn's going to put a silver lining on this situation.

00:46:06.460 --> 00:46:06.840
 Sorry, go ahead.

00:46:10.710 --> 00:46:14.670
 But in the conversation around creating language, this is another part of it.

00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:20.270
 This vocabulary, this lexicon of the kitchen is expanding people's palates.

00:46:22.570 --> 00:46:25.120
 In a very sensorial way.

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:30.980
 Because we have the opportunity to engage folks, again, in that place of getting a little

00:46:30.980 --> 00:46:35.100
 uncomfortable in order for a shift to happen in someone's experience of the world.

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:38.900
 To not only engage with flavors that might be different, which, you know, you can do that

00:46:38.900 --> 00:46:39.300
 easily.

00:46:39.460 --> 00:46:42.040
 Just going around to any restaurant to find variety.

00:46:42.040 --> 00:46:49.800
 But for it to be a story-based and participatory experience of eating food.

00:46:50.140 --> 00:46:52.840
 And again, to sort of insist on it being so.

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:57.670
 That you don't take a bite without actually knowing this story of this dish.

00:46:58.090 --> 00:47:02.610
 It creates an opportunity for someone to learn something new about their palate.

00:47:02.610 --> 00:47:10.720
 And about where their assumptions have been established when it comes to the flavors that

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:13.940
 they experience, the textures, even the visual experience.

00:47:14.320 --> 00:47:19.880
 The menu that was done for Farm was inspired and actually completely created by Norma Listman.

00:47:20.700 --> 00:47:25.510
 And this menu was, I can't even, it was an epiphany.

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:26.710
 It was its own prophecy.

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:30.870
 Because, you know, there was this idea of seeds throughout the meal, right?

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:36.250
 And the packages of seeds, you know, transporting of seeds in different ways across the world.

00:47:36.840 --> 00:47:41.600
 And each of her dishes was sort of the, evoke this idea of parcel.

00:47:41.940 --> 00:47:43.800
 And for me, that was a new lexicon.

00:47:43.800 --> 00:47:50.960
 That was not taking an easy route to have an experience of, you know, traditional Mexican

00:47:50.960 --> 00:47:55.220
 food, which just feels like some version of that you could get somewhere else.

00:47:55.580 --> 00:48:02.880
 It was the opportunity to find out your place in the story of this food by taking the time

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:03.440
 to learn it.

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:09.030
 I just love that there are those moments of pause in our meals where we are giving an

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:14.770
 opportunity for folks to really find new levels of participation in a meal.

00:48:14.770 --> 00:48:15.830
 And what that can be.

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:16.940
 Makes me happy.

00:48:17.720 --> 00:48:19.520
 You know, like, language is like recipes, right?

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:25.730
 So this idea of, like, finding new words is also this idea of, like, finding words that

00:48:25.730 --> 00:48:28.870
 we used to have and language we used to have that's been taken from us.

00:48:28.950 --> 00:48:32.010
 And much like that, it's finding the recipes that we used to have.

00:48:32.250 --> 00:48:32.650
 Reclaiming.

00:48:32.690 --> 00:48:33.730
 And reclaiming, right?

00:48:34.170 --> 00:48:35.250
 And colonizing, yeah.

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:35.530
 Yeah.

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:40.140
 Because for us, you know, the recipes that when they're passed from generation to generation,

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:45.770
 they're also passing down what that generation went through, how those recipes were created,

00:48:45.850 --> 00:48:46.610
 what informed them.

00:48:46.650 --> 00:48:51.450
 When you look at our recipes, they tell us so much about who we are as a people, as a community.

00:48:51.810 --> 00:48:52.480
 It does.

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:55.140
 But they're nothing without aunties, you know?

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:55.880
 Right?

00:48:56.540 --> 00:48:56.640
 Yeah.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:00.500
 It's like, yeah, your auntie gave you your recipe, but you don't mess that up unless you

00:49:00.500 --> 00:49:05.200
 have your mom's or your auntie's presence with you in the kitchen, giving you that, oh,

00:49:05.220 --> 00:49:06.180
 no, this is the hand feel.

00:49:06.500 --> 00:49:09.480
 And that's what I wanted to say about the salt.

00:49:09.680 --> 00:49:10.040
 Incredible.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:10.400
 Right?

00:49:10.520 --> 00:49:16.760
 It's that at food, not bombs, in this proverbial food, not bombs meal that we ate that tastes

00:49:16.760 --> 00:49:18.780
 bad, nobody said it needed more salt.

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:19.660
 Right?

00:49:19.660 --> 00:49:25.590
 So, I think an act of love is actually sometimes saying it needs salt before it leaves this kitchen.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:30.640
 And how you say it needs salt, maybe is that auntie or something, but it's not...

00:49:30.640 --> 00:49:30.930
 I don't know.

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:31.570
 Aunties are mean.

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:32.450
 Yeah, aunties are mean.

00:49:32.630 --> 00:49:32.950
 I don't know.

00:49:33.840 --> 00:49:34.280
 Sometimes.

00:49:34.800 --> 00:49:35.840
 Sometimes it's not...

00:49:35.840 --> 00:49:36.520
 Yeah, no, no, no.

00:49:36.580 --> 00:49:37.460
 And they can be mean.

00:49:37.580 --> 00:49:38.960
 And it can happen like that, right?

00:49:38.980 --> 00:49:39.080
 Yeah.

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:41.690
 But they also love you and they remember your birthday.

00:49:42.080 --> 00:49:42.280
 Yeah.

00:49:42.420 --> 00:49:42.800
 You know?

00:49:43.420 --> 00:49:43.780
 Sometimes.

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:45.300
 Also, not always.

00:49:45.440 --> 00:49:46.220
 There's a lot of family.

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:50.740
 I like we're going in a very interesting direction, like shit-talking.

00:49:51.920 --> 00:49:53.330
 Well, we're just...

00:49:53.330 --> 00:49:57.710
 With invocation and reference and a little shit-talking all the time it goes together.

00:49:57.910 --> 00:49:58.950
 That's the vacation.

00:49:59.230 --> 00:49:59.770
 That is family.

00:49:59.850 --> 00:50:00.030
 Yeah.

00:50:00.230 --> 00:50:00.630
 Actually.

00:50:02.070 --> 00:50:03.370
 No, but it's very human, right?

00:50:03.450 --> 00:50:03.550
 Yeah.

00:50:03.550 --> 00:50:04.910
 I mean, that's this thing, right?

00:50:04.910 --> 00:50:07.390
 Where none of it's simple that way, right?

00:50:07.550 --> 00:50:11.970
 So, well, I mean, because to be in a kitchen, I would love to cook with you guys someday.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:12.910
 I want to come and volunteer.

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:17.030
 But to be in a kitchen where no one's in charge, most kitchens I've worked in, someone is clearly

00:50:17.030 --> 00:50:18.270
 in charge, right?

00:50:18.310 --> 00:50:21.890
 And there's a lot of the yes, chef kind of thing going on.

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:25.800
 And that's a whole road to go down in terms of weird in many ways.

00:50:25.900 --> 00:50:28.080
 And it definitely invisibilizes people, right?

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:28.800
 It definitely...

00:50:28.800 --> 00:50:30.860
 There's definitely a hierarchy in all of those things.

00:50:30.860 --> 00:50:35.430
 But also, it doesn't taste like all those hands have touched it, right?

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:39.430
 There's a very clear process to make sure that it tastes like one hand has touched it, and

00:50:39.430 --> 00:50:40.650
 that's the hand at the top.

00:50:40.750 --> 00:50:42.290
 That's the chef, right?

00:50:42.390 --> 00:50:47.970
 And so, if you're not doing that, somebody's still got to taste that before it gets out there.

00:50:48.210 --> 00:50:50.610
 And that's a lesson that we have learned, you know?

00:50:50.610 --> 00:50:50.890
 Many times.

00:50:50.890 --> 00:50:53.960
 I mean, you know, because there's times when that hasn't happened.

00:50:54.040 --> 00:50:56.170
 But I think that it is this...

00:50:56.170 --> 00:51:01.560
 It's how do you hold something enough so that you can be accountable to it and for it?

00:51:01.620 --> 00:51:03.280
 Because I think accountability is really important.

00:51:03.770 --> 00:51:08.770
 But not so tightly that you prevent someone else from participating in it.

00:51:09.010 --> 00:51:12.550
 So, it's like, you know, how do you balance those two things?

00:51:12.550 --> 00:51:16.330
 And I think that in not just the food, but in all of the different parts of, like, programming

00:51:16.330 --> 00:51:21.230
 the meals or creating, you know, all of the, like, the materials that go along with it,

00:51:21.410 --> 00:51:23.410
 it's that.

00:51:23.530 --> 00:51:26.890
 It's like, you know, how do we each claim, like, if something goes down, you can raise

00:51:26.890 --> 00:51:29.370
 your hand and be like, I'm really sorry about that.

00:51:29.550 --> 00:51:31.030
 Let's figure out how to get through this.

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:36.810
 But not so much that you are, quote-unquote, running the show, you know?

00:51:37.320 --> 00:51:39.590
 And it's tricky.

00:51:41.190 --> 00:51:42.090
 Because the kitchen's definitely...

00:51:42.090 --> 00:51:43.430
 I mean, it's definitely not a free-for-all.

00:51:43.430 --> 00:51:47.550
 Like, there's still a lot of planning and direction that's happening when we're cooking

00:51:47.550 --> 00:51:47.950
 together.

00:51:48.110 --> 00:51:52.170
 And there's still people who are, I feel like, underwriting each of the dishes that know what

00:51:52.170 --> 00:51:54.070
 the dish is supposed to be like and taste like.

00:51:54.490 --> 00:51:56.710
 We still want lots of hands in it, but we don't want to...

00:51:56.710 --> 00:51:56.810
 But it's me touring.

00:51:57.130 --> 00:52:00.730
 And we don't want the recipe to take a left turn, right?

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:02.610
 And end up to be something completely different.

00:52:03.520 --> 00:52:03.740
 Yeah.

00:52:04.140 --> 00:52:07.100
 And one of the things that's come up a lot, actually, is our...

00:52:07.100 --> 00:52:10.840
 In the recent meals is, like, you know, how do we be inclusive?

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:18.590
 And, you know, we are super diverse, you know, in terms of where our experiences and our flavors

00:52:18.590 --> 00:52:19.870
 and that we grew up with are.

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:24.650
 But, you know, how do we not, like, kind of kitchen sink that into each meal so that it

00:52:24.650 --> 00:52:30.070
 becomes this kind of, like, not spicy enough, not savory enough, not sweet enough kind of,

00:52:30.430 --> 00:52:32.230
 like, you know, like, then you end up with beige, you know?

00:52:32.350 --> 00:52:32.510
 Yeah.

00:52:32.590 --> 00:52:40.310
 So, like, and so one of, you know, we've talked about bringing in, like, a guest chef

00:52:40.310 --> 00:52:44.740
 to really kind of hold more, you know, in each of those because it's important to also

00:52:44.740 --> 00:52:49.460
 be culturally specific and to adapt it to the world and the time that we live in with

00:52:49.460 --> 00:52:52.000
 the farmers that are growing the food in California at this point, you know?

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:54.280
 So it's this kind of dance all the time.

00:52:54.380 --> 00:52:58.590
 But I just, you know, it's, yeah, it's like I feel like the place that we don't want to

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:05.410
 end up is where it just kind of all becomes this, like, blandy-bland, kind of, like, fusion-y

00:53:05.410 --> 00:53:16.320
 thing that has no accountability to an origin or a root, you know, or, you know, to, yeah,

00:53:16.500 --> 00:53:17.260
 or to a story.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:18.340
 To a story, exactly.

00:53:19.920 --> 00:53:24.990
 It's really interesting as we're, you know, as, again, we've been sort of teasing out how

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:29.710
 we can be present in this space for each of these, you know, events that we do.

00:53:30.410 --> 00:53:40.240
 Um, food is primary, but it's also the thing that, and we, we trust it as far as a, as

00:53:40.240 --> 00:53:45.560
 a catalyst and as a through point to all of our, all the experiences that we create.

00:53:46.240 --> 00:53:50.910
 And we noticed the necessity of us to step away from it at some point and for it to be

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:51.690
 what it is.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:57.190
 And then for us to cultivate the space, uh, without our presence to cultivate this space

00:53:57.190 --> 00:54:01.610
 beyond the food, it feels like that moment that you're talking about, as far as the experience

00:54:01.610 --> 00:54:05.930
 that you have, it is as much as we want to create that for other folks, we also want to

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:07.510
 create that for ourselves, you know?

00:54:07.790 --> 00:54:13.610
 And so there is that need at some point in the evening for us to let the food be, uh, and

00:54:13.610 --> 00:54:16.670
 let it stand on its own with, you know, our, all of our folks that are supporting that.

00:54:16.670 --> 00:54:22.840
 And for us to give the message for us to share all the things that went into, you know, the

00:54:22.840 --> 00:54:26.300
 choices we made of what is that amuse-bouche and why are we doing that?

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:31.650
 Um, what is this detail or that tea del, you know, what is the purpose, uh, behind this meal?

00:54:31.670 --> 00:54:34.430
 And what's your, what's the thing that you're going to carry away at the end?

00:54:34.490 --> 00:54:38.510
 You know, these pieces of collateral that Sita often creates for a meal, they stand alone,

00:54:38.510 --> 00:54:42.990
 but they also are given even more life when Sita is able to speak her story about it.

00:54:43.110 --> 00:54:47.490
 So there is that moment where we do almost a shift of all these other pieces need to be

00:54:47.490 --> 00:54:52.570
 amplified and the messaging needs to occur so that everything else resonates in a really

00:54:52.570 --> 00:54:54.650
 tangible and an intentional way.

00:54:54.650 --> 00:54:59.030
 And, you know, like I said, we've done several projects together and we get more and more nuanced

00:54:59.030 --> 00:55:04.290
 about how that happens and, you know, where it's important for us to show up and win, but

00:55:04.290 --> 00:55:09.960
 it's been increasingly made clear that it is not only something that we enjoy to do, but

00:55:09.960 --> 00:55:14.620
 it is something that's appreciated by the guests at the table to really weave everything

00:55:14.620 --> 00:55:18.560
 together in a story that can be taken out of the room with them.

00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:24.050
 There's so much, we talk a lot, you know, we vision a lot, you know, there's so much, uh,

00:55:24.050 --> 00:55:25.890
 in the process of creating something.

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:32.450
 And I'm so grateful when, like you say, you can find that moment of resonance that stays

00:55:32.450 --> 00:55:37.060
 with you after everything else is done, after you, you know, you walk away from the table

00:55:37.060 --> 00:55:40.400
 and into your life, you know, it's pretty powerful.

00:55:43.870 --> 00:55:45.030
 I think we should leave it there.

00:55:45.770 --> 00:55:46.510
 Thanks guys.

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:47.630
 Thank you.

00:55:47.770 --> 00:55:48.630
 Thank you so much.

